  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Ratified in 1868, the 14th Amendment revolutionized American law by granting citizenship to all born or naturalized in the US (including former slaves), prohibiting states from denying due process, and guaranteeing equal protection under the law. It redefined American citizenship and dramatically expanded federal authority over civil rights. Key Provisions of the 14th Amendment Citizenship Clause: Establishes birthright citizenship, ensuring that all persons born or naturalized in the United States and subject to its jurisdiction are citizens of the United States and the state they reside in. Privileges or Immunities Clause: Prevents states from making or enforcing laws that abridge the privileges or immunities of citizens of the United States. Due Process Clause: Prohibits state governments from depriving any person of life, liberty, or property without due process of law. Equal Protection Clause: Requires states to provide equal protection of the law to all people within their jurisdiction, which has been the basis for major anti-discrimination court decisions. Historical Context and Significance Reconstruction Amendment: Passed after the Civil War to protect the rights of newly freed enslaved people and override the Dred Scott v. Sandford decision. Expansion of Rights: It extended the protections of the Bill of Rights to apply to state governments, not just the federal government. Legal Landmark: It has been the basis for numerous landmark Supreme Court cases, including Brown v. Board of Education (racial discrimination), Roe v. Wade (reproductive rights), and Bush v. Gore (election recounts). Insurrection Clause: Section 3 prevents individuals who have engaged in insurrection or rebellion against the U.S. from holding state or federal office. Key Court Interpretations Birthright Citizenship: Interpreted to include children born in the U.S. of resident aliens. Equal Protection: Used to combat discrimination and ensure fair treatment for all people, including non-citizens. Incorporation Doctrine: Used to apply the Bill of Rights to state governments.",
